  "final_critic_review_md": "## Disposition: accept\n\n**The exact candidate solves the original problem, preserves a rigorous argument from the baseline, and satisfies the LaTeX contract.** I reread both arguments, checked the principal cited inputs and their hypotheses, and independently compiled the candidate.\n\nThe earlier required corrections are present. I found no lost proof step, substantive regression, weakened conclusion, or remaining essential gap.\n\n## 1. Status of the earlier findings\n\n| Earlier finding | Current assessment |\n|---|---|\n| Rationality must refer to \\([\\sigma'/(2\\pi)]\\) | **Resolved.** Step 2 now explicitly uses this normalization, consistently with the curvature convention. |\n| The metric used for polar decomposition was implicit | **Resolved.** \\(g=\\sigma(\\cdot,K\\cdot)\\) is specified. |\n| The abstract overstated the monomial-transition conclusion | **Resolved.** It now describes transitions between nested systems. |\n| The sentence after the monomial lemma had incorrect quantifier wording | **Resolved.** The implication is now correctly stated for each fixed \\(J\\). |\n| Uniform estimates apply to \\(F_a^Bdf_a^B\\), not necessarily to \\(df_a^B\\) alone | **Resolved.** The strategy accurately describes the frame-weighted differentials. |\n| Remaining \\(A/\\Phi\\) notation mismatch | **Resolved.** The graph-map norm is consistently written \\(\\|\\Phi\\|\\). |\n| Dependencies of \\(C_2\\) | **Resolved.** \\(C_1\\) is included. |\n| Incorrect pointer for the no-\\((n+1)\\)-small-sections assertion | **Resolved.** The proof now cites the choice of \\(\\delta_0\\) preceding the cover lemma. |\n| The introductory adapted-\\(J\\) assertion needed the regularization qualification | **Resolved.** That qualification is explicit. |\n\nThe earlier improvements concerning **closed intersections**, fixed bundle twists, the scale \\(N=qk\\), and the inclusion \\(W_x\\subset O_{B_x}\\) also remain intact.\n\nThe retained `%% FLAG` identifies a deduction requiring verification; it is not itself evidence of a gap. My assessment of that deduction follows below.\n\n## 2. Symplectic inputs and the denominator-uniformity issue\n\n### Regularization\n\nThe application of FMZ Theorem 2.13 is valid. In the specified arXiv version, its deformation has the form\n\\[\n\\omega_{t,\\tau}=\\omega_t+d\\mu_{t,\\tau}\n\\]\nand remains in the positive-configuration space. Taking the parameter space to be a point gives precisely the cohomologous path required here. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1410.0609v3))\n\nThe compatible-structure construction is also sound. After regularization, the normal splittings make the metric orthogonality conditions mutually consistent. These conditions are linear in the metric and survive partition-of-unity averaging. The descending extension over the closed intersections is legitimate in normal-crossing charts. Polar decomposition then preserves every branch tangent space.\n\n### Auxiliary divisors\n\nThe fixed twists \\(L\\otimes A^k\\) and \\((\\underline{\\mathbb C}\\oplus L)\\otimes A^k\\) fall within Auroux’s arbitrary-vector-bundle construction. ([people.math.harvard.edu](https://people.math.harvard.edu/~auroux/papers/ahsub4.pdf))\n\nMohsen’s relative theorem applies to arbitrary compact smooth submanifolds, and the fixed-vector-bundle extension is explicitly recorded. Thus the restriction-transversality step does not require \\(K'\\) to preserve the old intersections. ([afst.centre-mersenne.org](https://afst.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/afst.1612.pdf))\n\nThe candidate correctly retains separate transversality conditions for\n\\[\nu,\\qquad v,\\qquad (u,v)\n\\]\non every relevant closed intersection, including \\(X\\). The pair condition is essential and has not been lost. Successive sufficiently small perturbations preserve the previously obtained quantitative conditions; this is consistent with the cited globalization framework. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0010052))\n\n### Independence of the transversality bound from \\(q\\)\n\n**I find the manuscript’s deduction valid.** It is appropriately presented as a consequence of the construction, not as a verbatim uniform-in-polarization theorem.\n\nWith \\(N=qk\\), the relevant length scale is \\(N^{-1/2}\\). In local unitary gauges, the connection and peak-section estimates are controlled by the compact family of background forms and structures; the fixed twist contributes lower-order terms. The restriction estimate is uniform in the real tangent subspace, while globalization uses normalized derivative bounds and separation counts. These dependencies support choosing a common positive \\(\\eta\\) before choosing the rational approximation. ([afst.centre-mersenne.org](https://afst.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/afst.1612.pdf))\n\nConsequently, the subsequent bound\n\\[\nC\\|K'-K\\|+CN^{-1/2}\n\\]\npermits the stated order of choices: first make \\(K'\\) sufficiently close to \\(K\\), then increase \\(k\\). Symplecticity is thereby established for \\(\\sigma\\), not merely for \\(\\sigma'\\).\n\nNo identification or gauge equivalence between polarizing bundles with different denominators is needed. The rational form remains construction data and is never inserted into the asserted deformation path.\n\n## 3. End-to-end audit of the remaining argument\n\n### Assumptions and conclusion\n\nThe positive-SNC convention and the meaning of “smooth complex coordinates” agree with the baseline. The theorem retains the full requested conclusion:\n\n- one global tame almost complex structure;\n- defining functions holomorphic throughout neighborhoods;\n- local equations for every point, including points outside \\(D\\);\n- a path cohomologous at every time and preserving the original SNC collection.\n\nThe argument does not substitute branch invariance or normal integrability for actual holomorphic defining functions.\n\n### Linear algebra\n\nThe Hermitian-form sign computation is correct, and negative graph subspaces give tame structures without an additional compatibility symmetry condition. The cited contraction parametrization and polar-decomposition pointers are appropriate. ([arxiv.org](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1011.1690v2))\n\nThe holomorphicity constraints are affine in the graph map. Thus the proof correctly averages graph maps, not the endomorphisms \\(J\\).\n\nThe local construction\n\\[\n\\mu=-RQ\n\\]\nsatisfies \\(Q+P\\mu=0\\). The pseudoinverse is smooth on the surjective locus and has the required minimum-norm property. Compactness supplies the uniform tame neighborhood.\n\n### Forest and normal derivatives\n\nThe forest induction includes every previously constructed divisor at each application of the auxiliary lemma. The count \\(m(2^n-1)\\) is correct.\n\nThe normal-linearization argument preserves both zero sets and transversality. The orientation statement gives\n\\[\n|A|>|B|\n\\]\nfor the real-linear normal derivative \\(z\\mapsto Az+B\\bar z\\); compactness supplies the positive uniform margin needed to dominate the quadratic remainder.\n\nThe sections can be modified independently because only bundle isomorphisms—not identities between sections—are retained. The candidate makes this dependency clearer than the baseline.\n\n### Cover and monomial transitions\n\nThe cover argument handles the strict and non-strict threshold inequalities correctly. An accessible small leaf would produce \\(n+1\\) simultaneously small sections, explaining why depth \\(n\\) suffices.\n\nThe monomial lemma is valid on the stated enlarged domain \\(O_A\\cap O_B\\). Every inverted boundary factor is nonzero there. The recursion\n\\[\nr_a=\\frac{r_{a+}}{r_{a-}}\n\\]\ncancels the same bundle isomorphism in numerator and denominator, leaving no arbitrary smooth coefficient.\n\nThus the enlarged domain is a justified strengthening, not an editorial overreach.\n\n### Uniform estimates\n\nThe three uniform facts follow from compactness, transversality, and complex linearity of the normal derivatives.\n\nThe decisive identity remains\n\\[\n\\lambda_a^B=F_a^Bdf_a^B\n=\\nabla_as_a-s_a\\beta_a^B.\n\\]\nThe logarithmic-derivative estimate depends only on terminal boundary sections and fixed connection-discrepancy forms. It does not require bounds on the magnitudes of the recursive frames.\n\nThe threshold choice\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_d=t^{\\,n-d}\\varepsilon_n\n\\]\ntherefore controls the error uniformly. The right-inverse perturbation argument and the estimate for \\(Q_B^\\lambda\\) are correct. Constants are fixed before the thresholds; there is no circular choice.\n\nThe local structure extends to an open neighborhood by strict smallness and surjectivity, rather than existing only at the selected point.\n\n### Master systems and patching\n\nThe union defining \\(B_x\\) satisfies both its upper bounds and its boundary lower bounds. The no-\\((n+1)\\)-small-sections property applies to the union itself, so \\(|B_x|\\le n\\).\n\nFiniteness permits the simultaneous shrinking of \\(W_x\\). On every relevant overlap, the nested monomial identities transfer holomorphicity from the master functions to the functions belonging to \\(U_A\\).\n\nAt a point of \\(U_A\\), every contributing local structure consequently satisfies the same affine constraints. Their convex patching preserves those constraints exactly. No derivatives of partition functions occur.\n\n### Coordinates and deformation\n\nAt a point of the original divisor, all corresponding roots are active. The selected functions have jointly surjective real differential, because multiplication by their nonzero frames preserves the original transversality.\n\nCoordinate completion and shrinking away from branches not containing the point yield the required local equation. The complement of \\(D\\), and the case \\(D=\\varnothing\\), are covered.\n\nFinally, every actual form change is an exact regularization deformation preserving all existing divisors. Finite concatenation with smooth endpoint reparametrizations supplies the stated path. Nothing in the final conclusion is weaker than the original problem allows.\n\n## 4. Clarity and LaTeX contract\n\nThe introduction, explicit orientation discussion, and named intermediate propositions make the proof easier to follow than the baseline. The strategy paragraph is dense but accurate. The trimming reported by the editor has not removed a necessary argument.\n\nI reconstructed the pasted candidate and ran `pdflatex` three times until references stabilized.\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Complete standalone document | Pass |\n| Exactly `\\documentclass[12pt]{article}` | Pass |\n| At most 16 pages | **Pass: 15 pages** |\n| Permitted layout only | Pass |\n| No prohibited spacing or font-size commands | Pass |\n| Successful compilation | Pass |\n| Resolved references and citations | Pass |\n\nThe final log contains two small overfull boxes, approximately **3.24 pt** and **1.12 pt**, and one underfull bibliography box. They cause no clipping and are cosmetic, not contract violations.\n\n**No corrective edit, restored passage, or new mathematical lemma is required.**\n\n<cleanup_verdict>accept</cleanup_verdict>\n\n",
